<h1>why do some graduates get jobs faster with worse grades</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">Employers hire for proof of work, not proof of patience, because the filter system that rewards grades measures time served and test-taking compliance while the hiring system that moves fast values demonstrated skill, referrals, and signal that survives a real task. The graduate with worse grades and a portfolio, internship, or warm intro is bringing evidence the recruiter can actually use.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>An internship cuts the odds of underemployment by <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">48.5%</a>, per Strada Institute and Burning Glass Institute: the experience signal outweighs the transcript signal.</li>
    <li>Resume use fell to <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">67%</a> in 2025, down from 73%, as employers bypass the credential proxy for direct evaluation.</li>
    <li>The speed advantage comes from bypassing the credential queue entirely: a referred candidate with a GitHub repo or a scored project skips the GPA screen that slows down the "strong on paper" applicant.</li>
    <li>Grades measure solo, timed, theoretical performance under artificial constraints, the exact profile AI is replacing in the workplace.</li>
    <li>The worst position is strong grades with no parallel signal: you win the filter, then stall in the funnel where the filter no longer matters.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>what grades actually measure (and why employers stopped trusting them)</h2>
  <p>Grades measure your ability to absorb information, regurgitate it under pressure, and comply with arbitrary deadlines. These are real traits, and they correlate weakly with conscientiousness. But they do not measure whether you can write production code, manage a stakeholder, close a sale, or ship a campaign.</p>
  <p>The filter operating system that produces grades was built for a different economy. Universities are time-based filters: progress by waiting a year and not failing. The score itself is secondary to the filtration function. Because the system filters rather than educates, the content drifts toward what is easy to test, not what is valuable to do.</p>
  <p>Employers know this. The shift to skills-based hiring is not ideological, it is practical: <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers</a> now use skills-based hiring, up from 81% the year before. They have learned that a 3.9 GPA does not predict who will debug under pressure, handle an angry client, or scope an ambiguous project. Those skills emerge from doing the work, not from studying for the work.</p>
  <p>The graduate with worse grades who gets hired faster has usually built something else that functions as a proxy for ability: a summer internship where they shipped real output, a side project with users, a referral from someone who vouched for their execution. The employer sees that signal and moves. The high-GPA applicant without parallel proof enters the same queue as everyone else, and the queue is slow.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>the three fast-lane signals that beat the transcript</h2>
  <p>Speed-to-hire is not random. The graduates who move faster, grades aside, tend to carry one or more of three signals that compress the hiring timeline.</p>
  <p><strong>Proof of work.</strong> A portfolio, GitHub repository, case study, or scored project that the hiring manager can evaluate in minutes. This collapses the typical multi-round screening process because the evidence is visible before the interview starts. The candidate with a live product and metrics to discuss is not guessing at what the interviewer wants, they are showing what they have already done.</p>
  <p><strong>Warm introduction.</strong> A referral from someone the hiring manager trusts transfers trust directly, bypassing the credential filter entirely. This is the social layer of hiring that the meritocratic narrative ignores. The referred candidate gets a conversation, not an application portal. The conversation converts faster because it operates on relationship, not on ranking.</p>
  <p><strong>Internship conversion.</strong> The intern who performed well is already inside the evaluation window. <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/talent-acquisition/internships/intern-conversion-rate-hits-highest-mark-in-five-years">63.1% of interns</a> converted to full-time in 2024-25, the highest rate in five years. The employer has already observed their work in context. Grades become irrelevant because the more predictive signal, actual performance in a role-like environment, already exists.</p>
  <p>The common thread: all three signals are <em>observed</em> rather than <em>asserted</em>. A grade is an assertion by an institution about your potential, while proof of work, a referral, and internship performance are observations by the employer or their network about your actual output. Observed signal moves faster because it reduces uncertainty.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>why the high-GPA, no-signal applicant stalls</h2>
  <p>The graduate with excellent grades and nothing else is optimized for a stage of the process that increasingly gets skipped. They win the resume screen, or they would if employers still screened that way. But the screen itself is losing priority.</p>
  <p>Resume use fell to <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">67%</a> in 2025. More employers are using work samples, structured assessments, and direct evaluation. The high-GPA applicant enters a system that no longer weights the credential heavily, and they have not built the alternative signals that would carry them through the new filter.</p>
  <p>There is a subtler problem: the psychology of the high-GPA track. The filter system rewards compliance, risk aversion, and single-threaded focus on the institutional metric. The student who spent four years optimizing for grades may have underinvested in the messy, ungraded work that builds actual proof: side projects, networking, internships that do not fit the semester calendar, rough drafts shipped to real users. They are not less capable. They are less legible to a hiring system that has moved on from the metric they optimized for.</p>
  <p>The result is a cruel inversion. The graduate who ignored the grading game, or who had lower grades because they were building something else, arrives at the job market with the signal that now matters, while the graduate who won the grading game arrives with a trophy from a tournament employers stopped watching.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>the structural shift: from pedigree to scored work</h2>
  <p>This is not a moral story about hard work versus entitlement. It is a structural story about what happens when the credential that once guaranteed a look loses its guarantee.</p>
  <p>Universities sell degrees like mortgages: you borrow to buy an asset, then pay it off for years. But the asset has depreciated for employers because the value is in skill, not in degree. The degree still carries status value, and some employers still screen by it, but the trend is toward direct evaluation of what you can do.</p>
  <p>The faster hire with worse grades is often the early adapter to this new reality. They recognized, consciously or not, that the filter system and the hiring system were diverging, and they placed their effort where the hiring system was heading. They built proof. They got referred. They interned. They did not waste energy on a signal that was losing its purchasing power.</p>
  <p>The deeper question is what replaces the old signal. The answer is scored work: actual tasks, evaluated against a professional bar, with feedback that improves the next submission. This is what the hiring system wants, and it is what the education system still refuses to build. The gap is where zero operates.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what this means for students still in the filter</h2>
  <p>If you are currently optimizing for grades, the honest calibration is this: grades are necessary but not sufficient, and they are becoming less sufficient faster than most students realize. The safe path is no longer the high-GPA path alone. It is the high-GPA path plus parallel proof.</p>
  <p>The practical move is to treat grades as a baseline maintenance task, not as the primary investment of your time. Allocate serious hours to building something that survives outside the grading context: a project with users, an internship with deliverables, a public body of work that a stranger can evaluate. The goal is to become legible to the hiring system that is replacing the old one.</p>
  <p>The graduate who gets hired faster with worse grades is not lucky. They are early. They built for the economy that exists, not the one the filter system pretends still does.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>do employers actually ignore grades now?</h3>
    <p>Not entirely, but the trend is sharp. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">53% of employers</a> have dropped degree requirements, and resume use is falling. Grades still matter for graduate school, some consulting and finance screens, and early-career roles where no other signal exists. But they are no longer the dominant filter they were a decade ago.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>should i stop trying to get good grades?</h3>
    <p>No. Grades are a hygiene factor: bad grades can close doors, but excellent grades no longer open them the way they used to. The optimal strategy is to maintain strong enough grades to clear basic screens, then redirect the time you would have spent chasing a 4.0 toward building proof of work, networking, and internships.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>what counts as proof of work for non-technical roles?</h3>
    <p>Anything a hiring manager can evaluate without trusting an institution's proxy. For marketing: a campaign you ran, with metrics. For product: a case study of a problem you scoped and solved. For operations: a process you redesigned, with before-and-after. The form varies by field, but the principle is constant: observed output, not asserted potential.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>why do referrals beat applications so consistently?</h3>
    <p>Trust transfer. A referral imports the trust the hiring manager has in the referrer, collapsing the multiple rounds of evaluation that an unknown candidate must survive. The referred candidate is pre-vetted by someone whose judgment the employer already respects. This is why networking is not optional career advice, it is structural advantage in a system that runs on trust.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>is this just about tech, or all industries?</h3>
    <p>Tech is the leading edge, but the shift is broader. Skills-based hiring is spreading across healthcare, finance, logistics, and professional services. The industries that still weight grades most heavily are those with the most rigid credentialing gatekeeping: law, medicine, academia, and parts of consulting. Even there, the parallel signal of internships and referrals accelerates entry.</p>
  </div>
</section>

